## 7. Security Model

### Network Isolation
- Each student environment in separate K8s namespace
- NetworkPolicy blocks pod-to-pod traffic across namespaces
- Ingress only route students can use

### Compute Isolation
- Pods run as non-root user (uid 1000+)
- Linux capabilities dropped (NET_RAW, SYS_ADMIN, etc.)
- seccomp RuntimeDefault profile
- ResourceQuota prevents runaway resource consumption
- PID limit prevents fork bombs

### Authentication & Authorization
- JWT tokens with HMAC-SHA256 signing
- Session-based: session_id stored in JWT, validated against DB
- Token revocation via `revoked_at` flag (immediate logout)
- Refresh tokens hashed in DB (salted bcrypt)

### Data Protection
- S3 files: encrypted at rest (default SSE-S3)
- PostgreSQL: TLS connection from Go backend
- No secrets in environment variables (use K3s Secrets)

---

## 8. Monitoring & Operations

### Observability (MVP, minimal)
- Pod logs: `kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>`
- Deployment status: Query K8s API daily
- Failed environments: Background job marks with `failure_reason`
- Audit logs: Track user actions (optional Phase 2)

### Scaling Limitations
- Single EC2 medium: ~50 concurrent environments max (1 vCPU per 50 pods)
- If students exceed quota, new environments get queued
- Go backend auto-restarts failed pods (reconciler loop)

### Cost Optimization
- Idle timeout (30 min) saves compute
- No persistent storage (projects deleted when stopped)
- S3 storage is cheap (templates + artifacts)

